Subject: [PATCH] PermutationChains.c: invalid fopen() modes + dropped FILE*
         assignment make all solution output fail on Windows

PermutationChains.c writes no solution file at all when built with MSVC: the
process dies at the first solution with exit code 0xC0000409, which is normally
read as STATUS_STACK_BUFFER_OVERRUN (the /GS stack-cookie check), and which is
unaffected by /Od, /GS- or a larger stack. It is not a stack overrun. There are
two independent defects, both in the solution-writing block, and both invisible
on macOS/BSD libc:

1. Invalid fopen() mode strings: the code passes "wa" and "aa". A C mode string
   is a single leading 'r'/'w'/'a' optionally followed by '+', 'b', 't'; the
   trailing 'a' is not valid. BSD libc (macOS) inspects only the first character
   and silently ignores the rest, so the calls behave as the intended "w"/"a".
   The Microsoft UCRT validates the whole mode string, and an unrecognised
   character trips the invalid-parameter handler, whose default action is
   __fastfail(FAST_FAIL_INVALID_ARG). __fastfail raises status 0xC0000409 — the
   same code used for stack-cookie failures, which is why the real fault
   misreports as a stack buffer overrun. Reduced repro, which reproduces
   0xC0000409 exactly under `cl /O2` (fopen never returns):

       FILE *f = fopen("x.txt","wa");

   glibc and mingw-w64's msvcrt are a third case: they reject the mode and
   return NULL, so the program exits via its own "Error opening file" path.

2. A dropped assignment: the second fopen() discards its return value.

       fopen(superPermsOutputFile, ...);   /* result thrown away */
       if (f==NULL) ...
       printSuperPerm(f);
       fclose(f);

   `f` still holds the twoCycles stream, fclose()d three lines earlier. So the
   NULL check tests the wrong pointer, printSuperPerm() writes to a closed
   FILE*, fclose() double-closes it, and the stream fopen() actually returned is
   leaked — once per solution (42,288 times at n=6). This is undefined
   behaviour. It is masked on macOS only because BSD libc allocates FILE objects
   from a recycled pool: fclose() releases the slot and the immediately
   following fopen() hands back the very same struct, so the stale `f` happens
   to point at the newly opened file. A libc that does not recycle the slot
   writes the superpermutations into a closed stream and double-closes it.
   AddressSanitizer does not flag this, because the FILE object is never
   malloc/free'd from the sanitizer's point of view — which is why it survived
   sanitized runs.

Verification: with the patch, the clang/macOS output is byte-identical to the
unpatched build (n=5 -> 6 solutions, n=6 -> 42,288 solutions, same solution
files). MSVC and mingw-w64 Windows builds, which previously produced zero
solution files, now match macOS: on three n=7 nsk kernels the full search trace
is identical line-for-line between the Windows and macOS binaries apart from one
informational line reporting sizeof(long).

---
--- PermutationChains.c
+++ PermutationChains.c
@@ -2358,7 +2358,7 @@
 printf("Found SOLUTION %d (size %d) among %d provisional solutions:\n",PCsolCount+1,PCsolSize,PCprovCount);
 if (showSols) showPCsol();

-FILE *f = fopen(twoCyclesOutputFile,twoCyclesOutputFileOpened?"aa":"wa");
+FILE *f = fopen(twoCyclesOutputFile,twoCyclesOutputFileOpened?"a":"w");
 if (f==NULL)
 	{
 	printf("Error opening file %s\n",twoCyclesOutputFile);
@@ -2378,7 +2378,7 @@
 fprintf(f,"}\n");
 fclose(f);

-fopen(superPermsOutputFile,superPermsOutputFileOpened?"aa":"wa");
+f = fopen(superPermsOutputFile,superPermsOutputFileOpened?"a":"w");
 if (f==NULL)
 	{
 	printf("Error opening file %s\n",superPermsOutputFile);
@@ -4707,7 +4707,7 @@

 if (twoCyclesOutputFileOpened)
 	{
-	f = fopen(twoCyclesOutputFile,"aa");
+	f = fopen(twoCyclesOutputFile,"a");
 	if (f==NULL)
 		{
 		printf("Error opening results file %s to append\n",twoCyclesOutputFile);
